    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "1. The numbers in the top box represent the midpoints of each interval.\n2. So, for $0$, it is the lower value, which is $0$.\n3. For $2z$, it is half the difference between $8z$ (the upper value) and $0$, which is $2(8z - 0) / 2 = 8z/2 = 4z$.\n4. For $4z$, it is half the difference between $16z$ (the upper value) and $8z$, which is $2(16z - 8z) / 2 = 16z/2 = 8z$.\n5. For $8z$, it is half the difference between $16z$ (the upper value) and $8z$. This gives us $16z/2 = 8z$.\n6. For $16z$, it is the upper value, which is $16z$.\n\n\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\textbf{Number} &0& 2z &4z &8z &16z \\\\\n\\hline\n\\textbf{Mean} &$0$ & $4z$ & $8z$ & $8z$ & $16z$\\\\ \\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n\nTherefore, the average value is $\\boxed{8z}$.\n\nThe average of a set of data, referring to the sum of all data divided by the count of data, is what value? In this data set, there are a total of 5 data points. The data is as follows: $0$, $2z$, $4z$, $8z$, and $16z$. The mean value of these data points is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{0 + 2z + 4z + 8z + 16z}{5} = \\frac{30z}{5} = 6z\n$$\n\nThis confirms that the average value of the given data set is $\\boxed{6z}$.",
